  <!-- 2. ABSTRACT -->
  <h1 class="first-title">2. Abstract</h1>
  <p><strong>DevTrack System</strong> is a production-grade full-stack issue and task management web application designed for modern software development environments. Software projects frequently suffer from fragmented bug reports, unassigned defects, and lack of status visibility when managed over informal communications or spreadsheets. DevTrack addresses this problem by providing a secure, centralized web application where team members can log issues, assign tasks to registered developers, update task resolution statuses (Open, In Progress, Resolved), assign priority levels (Low, Medium, High), search and filter issues in real time, and view workload metrics through interactive visual charts.</p>
  <p>The application is built using a clean 3-tier Model-View-Controller (MVC) architecture. The client interface is a single-page Vanilla HTML5/CSS3/JavaScript application featuring custom glassmorphic styling, persistent Light/Dark themes, and Chart.js analytics. The backend server is implemented in Node.js and Express.js, providing stateless JSON Web Token (JWT) authentication, bcrypt password hashing, input validation, and REST API endpoints. Data is stored in a relational MySQL database utilizing parameterized queries and SQL JOINs between issues and user rosters. The system is version-controlled on GitHub and deployed live on Render cloud infrastructure.</p>

  <!-- 3. INTRODUCTION -->
  <h1>3. Introduction</h1>
  <p>In software engineering, continuous issue tracking is critical for delivering high-quality products on schedule. Bugs, feature requests, and system task improvements must be logged accurately, assigned to appropriate developers, and monitored until resolution.</p>
  <p>DevTrack provides a complete digital workplace solution for software teams. It establishes a structured workflow where every issue receives a unique tracking ID, detailed description, category classification (Bug, Feature, Task, Improvement), priority tag, assigned developer owner, and lifecycle status tracking.</p>
  <p><strong>Real-World Use Case:</strong> An agile software development team working on a web application needs to record bug reports submitted by QA testers, assign them to frontend or backend engineers, and enable project managers to monitor daily resolution progress without manual status calls.</p>

  <!-- 4. PROBLEM STATEMENT -->
  <h1>4. Problem Statement & Technical Solution</h1>
  <div class="box">
    <strong>Official Problem Statement:</strong><br>
    "Software engineering teams frequently encounter communication delays, unassigned defect backlogs, and lost issue history when managing bugs across informal chat channels, emails, or spreadsheets. Modern software development requires a centralized, secure, and real-time issue management system to streamline developer assignment, enforce status transition visibility (Open, In Progress, Resolved), and deliver real-time visual analytics for project managers."
  </div>

  <h3>Problem ➔ DevTrack Solution Mapping</h3>
  <table>
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Problem in Traditional Workflow</th>
        <th>DevTrack Technical Solution</th>
        <th>How It Works in Code</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>1. Scattered Bug Reports</strong></td>
        <td>Centralized issue database storage with unique tracking IDs (`#1`, `#2`, etc.)</td>
        <td>MySQL database table `issues` stores title, description, timestamp, and reporter ID.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>2. Unassigned Responsibilities</strong></td>
        <td>Explicit developer assignment linked to team user roster</td>
        <td>Foreign Key `assignee_id` links task directly to registered developers with avatar previews.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>3. Zero Status Visibility</strong></td>
        <td>Strict Status badges (Open, In Progress, Resolved) with color indicators</td>
        <td>ENUM column `status` in MySQL drives UI badges (Red for Open, Amber for In Progress, Green for Resolved).</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>4. Priority Ambiguity</strong></td>
        <td>Priority tags (Low, Medium, High) with priority-based sorting</td>
        <td>SQL query orders rows by `FIELD(priority, 'High', 'Medium', 'Low')` to highlight urgent tasks.</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><strong>5. Lack of Workload Analytics</strong></td>
        <td>Interactive Donut and Bar distribution charts powered by Chart.js</td>
        <td>Backend route `/api/dashboard/stats` aggregates counts and returns data to HTML Canvas charts.</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <!-- 8. COMPLETE FEATURE LIST -->
  <h1>8. Complete Feature List</h1>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Authentication Engine</strong>: User registration, login, profile retrieval, logout, bcrypt encryption, JWT session storage in localStorage.</li>
    <li><strong>Overview Dashboard</strong>: 5 Stat Metric Cards (Total, Open, In Progress, Resolved, High Priority) and Recent Activity log.</li>
    <li><strong>Visual Analytics</strong>: Interactive Status Breakdown Donut Chart and Priority Distribution Bar Chart using Chart.js Canvas rendering.</li>
    <li><strong>Issue Management (CRUD)</strong>: Create issue modal, edit issue modal, view details modal, confirmation delete modal.</li>
    <li><strong>Status Management</strong>: Open (Red), In Progress (Amber), Resolved (Green) badges.</li>
    <li><strong>Priority Tagging</strong>: High (Red), Medium (Amber), Low (Blue) pill tags.</li>
    <li><strong>Category Classification</strong>: Bug, Feature, Task, Improvement tags.</li>
    <li><strong>Developer Team Roster</strong>: Assign registered team members to issues with avatar preview.</li>
    <li><strong>Live Keyword Search</strong>: Instant filtering on issue titles and descriptions.</li>
    <li><strong>Multi-Criteria Filters & Sorting</strong>: Filter by Status, Priority, Category, and sort by Date (Newest/Oldest), Priority, Title (A-Z).</li>
    <li><strong>Theme Manager</strong>: Persistent Dark/Light mode toggle with local storage memory.</li>
    <li><strong>Toast Notifications</strong>: Animated success/error notification banners.</li>
  </ul>

  <!-- 9. USER ROLES AND PERMISSIONS -->
  <h1>9. User Roles and Permissions</h1>
  <p>DevTrack implements role attributes stored in the <code>users</code> database table:</p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Project Manager</strong>: Manages issue priorities, assigns tasks to team developers, and monitors overall dashboard statistics.</li>
    <li><strong>Developer</strong>: Receives assigned issues, updates task statuses from Open to In Progress or Resolved, and logs technical details.</li>
    <li><strong>QA Tester</strong>: Identifies bugs, creates detailed bug reports, and verifies resolved issues.</li>
    <li><strong>Admin</strong>: Complete administrative oversight over users and issue records.</li>
  </ul>

